  logger's contract now, not retrofitted after the sink exists.

  - [1mCentral denylist in the logger[0m — the logger walks the context object it's handed and replaces values under keys like
    [38;5;153mpassword[39m, [38;5;153mtoken[39m, [38;5;153msecret[39m, [38;5;153mauthorization[39m with [38;5;153m[redacted][39m. Safe by default; a careless call site can't leak a
    password. Cost: a little traversal code, and it can still miss a secret stored under an unusual key name.
  - [1mCall sites stay disciplined[0m — logger passes everything through untouched; the rule is "never hand it a credential."
    Zero code, zero overhead, and exactly one slip away from posting passwords to a collector.
  - [1mBoth[0m — denylist as the backstop, plus the convention that call sites don't pass credentials anyway.

  I recommend the denylist (functionally: "both", since the convention costs nothing to also state). Defense in depth is
  worth ten lines when the failure mode is plaintext passwords in a log store.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · How should the logger handle credentials and other sensitive fields? → Central denylist (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Last question before I present the design. This repo has no tooling at all — no test runner, no linter, no [38;5;153mscripts[39m in
  [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m. Setting that up is cheapest now, before the logger exists, and it matters for this change specifically:
  redaction and level filtering are exactly the kind of logic that should have tests proving a password never survives a
  log call.

  Worth noting on cost: Node ships a built-in test runner ([38;5;153mnode:test[39m), so unit tests need [1mzero dependencies[0m — relevant
  given this project currently has none. A linter would be the first [38;5;153mnode_modules[39m in the repo.

